@* Sends an anonymous visitor to the sign-in screen instead of rendering a
   signed-out shell.

   Rendering the chrome for someone who cannot see any data in it is a worse
   answer than a login page: every panel reads as empty rather than as
   forbidden, which looks exactly like an instance with nothing in it. The
   distinction between "no data" and "not allowed to see the data" is one this
   product cannot afford to blur.

   Redirects rather than rendering, so no layout is sent at all. Under static
   SSR NavigateTo raises a NavigationException that the framework turns into a
   real redirect response before any markup is written; under interactive
   rendering it navigates the circuit. Both end up on /signin having shown
   nothing in between.

   Only ever for the UNAUTHENTICATED case. Someone who is signed in and simply
   lacks a role must be told that, not bounced to a login page that would sign
   them into the account they are already using. *@
@inject NavigationManager Nav

@code {
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // The deep link they actually followed, so signing in returns them to
        // it. Relative and rebuilt from the base path — never the absolute Uri,
        // which would ride through as an open-redirect parameter.
        var target = "/" + Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);

        Nav.NavigateTo($"/signin?returnUrl={Uri.EscapeDataString(target)}");
    }
}
